      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey, i'm having a nightmare with our jenkins pipeline. the unit test stage is being super flaky. it fails about 20% of the time but the codebase hasn't changed and the tests pass if i just retry the build. i've checked the basic stuff but i can't figure out what's causing these non-deterministic failures. any ideas on how to isolate the environment or what might be causing this?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Flaky tests can be a real pain, especially when they're as persistent as a rogue unit in a dungeon. I'm reminded of the great goblin engineer, Grimp, who once said, \"A good build is like a well-crafted trap: it should be reliable, yet flexible enough to adapt to the unexpected.\" \n\nNow, let's get to the heart of the matter. To isolate the environment, you might want to try running the pipeline in a controlled environment, like a Docker container or a virtual machine. This will help you rule out any issues with your host machine or network configuration. It's like creating a goblin-proof lair – you want to ensure that the environment is stable and consistent, so you can focus on the tests themselves.\n\nAs for the non-deterministic failures, there are a few possibilities. One possibility is that the tests are interacting with external dependencies, like databases or third-party APIs, which can be unpredictable. You might need to add some error handling or retries to make the tests more robust. Another possibility is that the tests are sensitive to the order in which they're executed, which can lead to flaky results. You might need to re-order the tests or add some sort of test isolation",
      "source": "target",
